\newcommand{\question}[1]{\textbf{ #1 }}

\begin{document}

\question{ Solve the following math problem step-by-step: }

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{(\alph*)}]
    \item Find the set of possible values for the whole numbers $x$ and $y$ between $1$ and $10$ inclusive for each partner.
    \begin{enumerate}[start=1, leftmargin=*, wide=0pt, labelwidth=!, labelindent=0pt]
        \item For the partner who picked $x$ and $y$: what is the set of possible values for their numbers which are strictly greater than both of yours?
        \item For the partner who picked $x-4$ and $2y-1$: what is the set of possible values they produced for their numbers which are also in your set of greater numbers from part (b)?
        \item For the partner who picked $x-4$ and $2y-1$: what is the final set of numbers from part (c) but after removing duplicates.
    \end{enumerate}

    \item \boxed{<final_answer_set>}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{(\alph*)}]
    \item We enumerate all possible values for $x$ and $y$:
    \begin{enumerate}[openparen, itemjoin=\\,leftmargin=2em]
        \item $x = \{2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10\}$, largest value $x_{max} = 10$
        \item $y$ can either follow or precede $x$, ranging from $1$ to $10$. \colorbox{gray!10}{\huge $\inlinestack{$\{ \colorbox{white}{1}, \colorbox{white}{2}, \cdots, \colorbox{white}{9}, \colorbox{white}{10}$\} $\colorbox{white}{ \ { 10,} \ 9, \cdots, 2, 1 \} $}}. To ensure there are at least 2 numbers, we discard the smallest pair: $x = 10$, $y = 1$. The largest pair is $x = 2$, $y = 10$.

        The cartesian product of the sets gives us all possibilities for $(x, y)$:

        \[\{(x, y)\} = \{(10, 1), (10, 2), \cdots, (10, 9), (10, 10), (9, 1), \cdots, (9, 10), \cdots, (2, 1), (2, 2), \cdots, (2, 10), (1, 1)\}\]

        With $^{10}C_{2} = 45$ pairs, counting the pair $(x, y) = (1, 1)$ only once.

    \item For each pair of numbers they picked in part (a), we consider the numbers greater than both:

        For each pair $(x_{i}, y_{i})$, the set of possible values $x'$ and $y'$ such that $x' > x_{i}$ and $y' > y_{i}$. Enumerate these:

        \[x' = \{x_{i}+1, \cdots, x_{max}\},\ y' = \{y_{i}+1, \cdots, y_{max}\}\]

        Produce the set of all greater numbers for each pair $(x_{i}, y_{i})$:

        \[\{(x' > x_i, y' > y_i)\} = \{(x' > x_{1,1}, y' > y_{1,1}), (x' > x_{1,2}, y' > y_{1,2}), \cdots, (x' > x_{10,10}, y' > y_{10,10}))\}\]

        \item From part (b), resolve the union of all greater number sets $(x' > x_i, y' > y_i)$:

        \[(x' > x_i, y' > y_i) \cup (x' > x_j, y' > y_j) \implies (\{x' > x_i\} \cup \{x' > x_j \}) \times (\{y' > y_i\} \cup \{y' > y_j\})\]

        \item In part (c), reduce to the set without duplicates:

        \[\{(x' > x_i, y' > y_i) \} \backslash \{ (x', y') \in (x' > x_j, y' > y_j) \}\]

        \item Compose the set of all the unique greater numbers:

        \[\{(x' > x_i, y' > y_i) \} = \{(x_{min}', y_{min}' \} \cup \{(x_{min}', y_{2}' \} \cup \cdots \cup (x_{max}', y_{max}')\}\]

        \item \boxed{<final_answer_set>}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{document}

The Latex code outputs the following result:

The final answer set $\{ (x_{min}', y_{min}' \} \cup \{(x_{min}', y_{2}' \} \cup \cdots \cup (x_{max}', y_{max}')\}$, which you can numerically work out based on the problem parameters. In this case, the set is $\{ (3', 3'), (3', 4'), \cdots, (10', 10'), (10', 11') \}$.

