Here is the problem in LaTeX:
\begin{align*}
\text{ Given circles with radius 1 centered at } (4,0) \text{ and } (-4,0) \text{, \\ if there exists tangent circles passing through } (0,5) \text{, find the coordinates of their centers.}
\end{align*}

We can solve this problem by first finding the equation of the line tangent to both circles and passing through $(0,5)$ and then finding the two centers of the tangent circles by using the properties of circles and tangent lines.

The slope of the line is given by the difference in the circular y-coordinates (center 1 minus center 2) divided by the difference in the x-coordinates (center 1 minus center 2).  So we first calculate the slope:
\begin{align*}
m &= \frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} = \frac{0-0}{4-(-4)} = \boxed{\frac{1}{8}}
\end{align*}

The equation of a line in the form y = mx + b can be found by choosing one of the points $(x_1, y_1)$ or $(x_2, y_2)$ and substituting them into the equation. We will use the point $(0,5)$ because it is easy to compute the value of b. So we have:
\begin{align*}
5 &= \boxed{\frac{1}{8}}(0) + b \\
\therefore \ b &= 5
\end{align*}

So the equation of the line is:
\begin{align*}
y &= \boxed{\frac{1}{8}}x + 5
\end{align*}
Now, to find the centers of the tangent circles with radius 1, we can use the equation of a circle centered at $(a,b)$ and of the form $(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = 1^2$:
\begin{align*}
(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 &= 1 \\
\therefore x^2 - 2ax + a^2 + y^2 - 2by + b^2 &= 1 \\
\end{align*}

We apply the equation of the line to the two circles and will get two equations that are equal:
\begin{align*}
\text{for center 1:} \ y &= \boxed{\frac{1}{8}}x + 5 \\
(x-4)^2 + (y-\boxed{0})^2 &= 1 \\
\text{for center 2:} \ y &= \boxed{\frac{1}{8}}x + 5 \\
(x+4)^2 + (y-\boxed{0})^2 &= 1
\end{align*}

Since the equations for center 1 and center 2 are equal, we can equate the coefficients on x and y:
\begin{align*}
\Longrightarrow x + 4 &= x - 4 \\
\Longrightarrow 8 &= 0 \\
\Longrightarrow \text{There is no valid pair of solutions for the centers.}
\end{align*}

In particular, there does not exist two circles having radius 1, tangent to both original circles, and passing through $(0,5)$.

