Step 1: By the Pythagorean theorem, we have $AE^2 + AE \cdot EC = AC^2$, so $AE^2 + AE \cdot 6 = 36$. Let's solve for $AE^2$:

\begin{align*}
AE^2 + 6AE &= 36 \\
AE^2 &= 36 - 6AE
\end{align*}

Step 2: Let $AE = x$. Thus, $x^2 = 36 - 6x$.

Step 3: Solving the quadratic equation $x^2 - 6x + 36 = 0$, we can find the two solutions for $x$: ($x_1$ and $x_2$). The foot of the altitude is at one of the two points, so we have:

\begin{align*}
&\boxed{ AE = \begin{cases}
x_{1} & \\
x_{2} &
\end{cases}}
\end{align*}

Step 4: To find the area of $\triangle AED$, we can use Heron's formula. First, we'll find the semi-perimeter ($s$):

\begin{align*}
s &= \frac{A + B + C}{2} = \frac{5 + 5 + 6}{2} = 7.5 \\
\end{align*}

Then, the area of $\triangle AED$ can be computed as:

\begin{align*}
& \text{area} \left(\triangle AED\right) = \sqrt{s \left(s-A\right)\left(s-B\right)\left(s-C\right)} \\
&                                = \sqrt{7.5 \left(7.5-5\right)\left(7.5-5\right)\left(7.5-6\right)} \\
&                                = \sqrt{7.5 \cdot 2.5 \cdot 2.5 \cdot 1.5} \\
&                                = \boxed{\sqrt{112.5} \text{ square units }}
\end{align*}

To find the area of $\triangle DEC$, we need to subtract the smaller of the two areas (if they exist) from the larger one. Finally:

\begin{align*}
\text{Area} \left(\triangle DEC\right) &= \text{area} \left(\triangle AED\right) - \text{min}(\text{area} \left(\triangle AED\right)) \\
&= \sqrt{112.5} \text{ square units} - \text{min}\left(\begin{cases}
\sqrt{112.5} \\
\text{NA}
\end{cases}\right) \\
&= \sqrt{112.5} \text{ square units}
\end{align*}

where NA means that the area is not applicable (i.e., when the two areas are the same and there's no smaller one to subtract).

