Step 1 - Solve the equation $x^2 - 2xy + 3y^2 = 5$ for $y$ in terms of $x$.
\begin{align*}
x^2 - 2xy + 3y^2 - 5 &= 0 \\
(x - y)^2 + 2y^2 - 5 &= 0 \\
y^2 &= \frac{5 - (x - y)^2}{2} \\
y &= \pm \sqrt{\frac{5 - (x - y)^2}{2}}
\end{align*}

Step 2 - Substitute $x=0$ into the solution from Step 1 to find the endpoints of the major (horizontal) axis.
\begin{align*}
y = \pm \sqrt{\frac{5}{(2)}}\approx \pm 2.24
\end{align*}

Step 3 - Substitute $y=\pm 2.24$ into the solution from Step 1 to find the endpoints of the minor (vertical) axis.
\begin{align*}
x - 2.24 &= \pm \sqrt{2(5 - (2.24)^2)}\approx \pm 3.51\text{ or }\pm 0.89
\end{align*}

Step 4 - Determine the points of intersection with the coordinate axes. Set $y=0$ to find the intersection with the $x$-axis:
\begin{align*}
x^2 - 2x \cdot 0 + 3 \cdot 0^2 = 5\\
x^2 = 5\\
x = \pm \sqrt{5}
\end{align*}
Then, set $x=0$ to find the intersection with the $y$-axis:
\begin{align*}
0^2 - 2 \cdot 0 \cdot y + 3 \cdot y^2 = 5 \\
0 + 3 \cdot y^2 = 5 \\
y^2 = \frac{5}{3} \\
y = \pm \sqrt{\frac{5}{3}}
\end{align*}

