Here's a basic approach to solve this problem:

1. Define the geometric series:
\[\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frac{1}{1 + \omega^k + \omega^{2k} + \omega^{3k}} = \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frac{1}{4\cdot (\omega^k + \omega^{2k})} = \frac{1}{4} \cdot \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \left(\frac{1}{\omega^k} + \frac{1}{\omega^{2k}}\right)\]

2. Show that the sum of the two series is equal:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \left(\frac{1}{\omega^k} + \frac{1}{\omega^{2k}}\right)
&= \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frac{1}{\omega^k} + \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frac{1}{\omega^{2k}} = \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \sum_{l = 0}^{1728-k} \underbrace{\omega^{-kon}}_{\text{sum over terms with power of omega}} \\
&= \omega^{-1} \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \sum_{l = 0}^{1728-k} \omega^{-k(l + 1)} + \omega^{-2} \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \sum_{l = 0}^{1728-k} \omega^{-2k(l + 1)}
\end{align*}

3. Show that the two sums above are the same:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} & \sum_{l = 0}^{1728-k} \omega^{-k(l + 1)} = \sum_{l = 0}^{1727} \sum_{k = 1}^{l+1} \omega^{-(k(l+1))} = \sum_{k=1}^{1728} \sum_{l = k}^{1728} \omega^{-cl} = \sum_{l = 1}^{1728} \sum_{k = l}^{1728} \omega^{-cl} = \sum_{l=1}^{1728} \frac{1-\omega^{-1728l}}{1-\omega^{-cl}} \\
&= \sum_{l=1}^{1728} \frac{1-\omega^{-1728l}}{1-\omega^{-1728}} = \frac{1}{1-\omega^{-1728}} \sum_{l=1}^{1728} (1-\omega^{-1728l}) = \frac{1-\sum_{l=1}^{1728} \omega^{-1728l}}{1-\omega^{-1728}}
\end{align*}

4. Express the sum in terms of the Jacobi theta functions $\vartheta_3(z)$ and $\vartheta_4(z)$:
Due to the preferred central derivation of the Jacobi theta functions, the values of $\omega$ and $1729$ need to be simplified as $\omega = e^{2 \pi i/n}$ and $n = 1729$ such that the m-th roots of unity are defined as $\zeta_m = e^{2 \pi i / m}$. Now we can express $\omega$ as
\[\omega = \zeta_n^{1+1088n} = \zeta_n^{1+12 \cdot 143}\]
Using the Jacobi triple product identity, we have
\begin{align*}
\vartheta_3(\omega) &= q^{1/2} + 2 \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} q^{k} \mathrm{cos} (2 \pi k z)\\
&= q^{1/2} + 2 \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{q^k} \left(\underbrace{\frac{q^k}{\sqrt{q}}\cdot \mathrm{cos}(2 \pi k z)}_{\text{$=q^{k/2}\cdot \mathrm{cos}(2 \pi k z ))$ since $q = e^{2 \pi i}}$ \right)\\
\end{align*}
Making the change of variable $t = 2 \pi kz$, we find
\begin{align*}
\vartheta_3(\omega) &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{q}} \sum_{t = 0}^{\infty} q^{t/2}\mathrm{cos}(t / z) \quad \text{with}\quad q = \omega^{-1} = e^{-2 \pi i / n}
\end{align*}
We get the closed-form summation we are looking for by calculating the summation along the boundaries of the fundamental domain of the lattice $\Gamma(n)$.
After some calculations, one can prove that
\begin{align*}
\sum_{l=1}^{1728} \omega^{-1728l} &= \sqrt{q}/ 2 \cdot \left(\vartheta_3(\omega) + \vartheta_4(\omega)\right) \\
&= \sqrt{q}/ 2 \cdot \left(1/\sqrt{q} + 2 \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{q^{2k}}\left(1 + \left(\frac{-1}{q}\right)^k\right)\right)\\
&= 1 + \sqrt{q} \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \left(\frac{-1}{q}\right)^{k} \quad\text{since}\quad =\sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{q^k} \left(\underbrace{\frac{\left(1/\sqrt{q}\right)^k + \left(-1/\sqrt{q}\right)^{k}}_{\text{$= 2\cdot 1 + \left(\frac{-1}{q}\right)^k$ since $q = e^{-2 \pi i / n}$}}$ \right) \ ,
\end{align*}

Now, we can finally compute the summation:
\begin{align*}
