\overline{BD}&=\frac{\overline{BA} sin x}{\sin z} \\
\text{Since } \overline{BD} &= \overline{EC}, \overline{CA} = \overline{AB}
\end{align}

In triangle EAC:
\begin{align}
\frac{\overline{EC}}{sin z} &=\frac{\overline{AE}}{sin y} \\
\overline{EC} &=\frac{\overline{AB} sin z}{sin y} \\
\text{Since } \overline{BD} &= \overline{EC}, \overline{AB} = 2R \text{ (where } R \text{ is the radius of the triangle's circumcircle)}
\end{align}

Combining expressions:
\begin{align}
\frac{\overline{BD}(sin y)}{sin z} = \frac{\overline{AE}}{sin y}
\end{align}

Now we can apply the law of sines to triangle ADE, where $\overline{AD} = R$ (radius of the circumcircle of triangle ABC):
