To convert the radian measurement back to degrees, you can multiply by 180/π. So first, convert the angles to degrees:

\begin{align*}
\text{degrees } 3 &= 3\times\frac{180}{\pi} \approx 53.13
\\
\text{degrees } 4 &= 4\times\frac{180}{\pi} \approx 71.56
\\
\text{degrees } 5 &= 5\times\frac{180}{\pi} \approx 89.98
\end{align*}

Now, for each angle, find the sine inverse in radians.  First, convert the angles back to radians:

\begin{align*}
\text{radians } 53.13 &= 53.13\times\frac{\pi}{180} \approx 0.927... \approx 0.927
\\
\text{radians } 71.56 &= 71.56\times\frac{\pi}{180} \approx 1.256... \approx 1.256
\\
\text{radians } 89.98 &= 89.98\times\frac{\pi}{180} \approx 1.57... \approx 1.571
\end{align*}

Now compute the sum:

\begin{align*}
\sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) &= \sin^{-1} (0.927) + \sin^{-1} (1.256) + \sin^{-1} (1.571)
\\
&= \text{radians } 60 \approx 1.047... + \text{radians } 78.69 \approx 1.357... + \text{radians } 90
\\
&= \text{radians } 229.69 \approx 127.56 \text{ radians }
\end{align*}

However, this answer exceeds the range of 0 to π for the sine function, so the result has a periodic repetition.  Examine the last part of the angle conversion:

\begin{align*}
\text{radians } 5 &= 5\times\frac{\pi}{180} - \text{ radians } 229.5 &= (5-229.5 ) \frac{\pi}{180}
\\
&= -\frac{234.5 \pi}{180}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the periodic repetition of the inverse sine has an argument of $-234.4 \pi / 180$. To account for this, we must subtract one period, $\pi$, from the result:

\begin{align*}
\sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) &= \text{radians } 229.69 \approx 127.56 \text{ radians } - \pi
\\
&= \text{radians }( 127.56-3.14 )  \approx \text{radians } 124.4
\end{align*}

Therefore $\sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) \approx 124.4 \text{ radians }.$ The angle back in degrees is

\begin{align*}
124.4 \text{ radians } \times \frac{180}{\pi} &\approx \boxed{71.87 \text{ degrees }}.
\end{align*}

