```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\begin{document}

The area of a square is equal to the area of the circle. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
A_\text{square} &= A_\text{circle} \\
(A_\text{square})^2 &= (A_\text{circle})^2
\end{align*}

The formula for the area of a circle is $A_\text{circle} = \frac{\pi d^2}{4}$, where $d$ is the diameter of the circle. Since the diametrical points divide the circle into two equal semicircles and four quadrants, the radius is half the diameter, i.e., $r = \frac{d}{2}$.

Substitute this into the area formula to get
\begin{align*}
A_\text{square}&= \left( \frac{\pi d^2}{4}\right)^2 = \frac{\pi^2 d^4}{16}
\end{align*}

The area of the square is given as 200 square points. 200 square points have the same length as one side of the square. Since 1 square point is equal to 1 unit, we can write:
\begin{align*}
A_\text{square} &= (1)^2 \cdot 200 = 200
\end{align*}

Now, go back to the equation for $A_\text{square}$ in terms of $\pi$, and solve for the diameter:
\begin{align*}
200 &= \frac{\pi^2 d^4}{16} \\
d^4 &= \frac{16 \times 200}{\pi^2}
\end{align*}
Cube root both sides to find the diameter:
\begin{align*}
d &= \left( \left( \frac{16 \times 200}{\pi^2}\right)^{1/4} \right)
\end{align*}

The diameter is the side length of a square, and each side of the square is divided into 200 equal parts. The length of one side is:
\begin{align*}
\text{Side Length} &= \frac{\text{Diameter}}{200}
\end{align*}

To find the number of squares, divide the diameter by the side length:
\begin{align*}
\text{Number of squares} &= \frac{\text{Diameter}}{\text{Side Length}}
\end{align*}

Now, substitute the expressions for Diameter and Side Length:
\begin{align*}
\text{Number of squares} &= \frac{\frac{\text{Diameter}}{200}}{\frac{\text{Diameter}}{2}} \\
\text{Number of squares} &= \frac{2}{\text{Diameter/200}}
\end{align*}

Finally, calculate the number of squares:
\begin{align*}
\text{Number of squares} &= \frac{2}{\left( \left( \left( \frac{16 \times 200}{\pi^2}\right)^{1/4} \right) / 200 \right)}
\end{align*}

\boxed{\frac{2\pi^2 \times 200^2}{16}}

You can simplify this result if desired, but the question asks for an exact numeric expression with units.

\end{document}
```

