Now that we have the number of quarters in the first three piles, we can find the number of quarters in the fourth pile. Since the fourth pile has twice as many quarters as the second pile:

\begin{align*}
\boxed{\text{quarters in the fourth pile}} &= 2 \times \boxed{\text{quarters in the second pile}} \\
\boxed{\text{quarters in the fourth pile}} &= 2 \times (\boxed{\text{quarters in the third pile} + \boxed{2}}) \\
