\cos 7 \theta &= \cos^7\theta - 14\cos^5\theta + 56\cos^3\theta - 70\cos\theta.
\end{align*}
We can set them equal to each other to form an equation in powers of $\sin\theta$:
\begin{align*}
3\sin\theta - 4\sin^3\theta = \cos^7\theta - 14\cos^5\theta + 56\cos^3\theta - 70\cos\theta.
\end{align*}
Next, we use double angle identities to express $\cos\theta$ as a function of $\sin\theta$. The double angle identity for cosine is:
\begin{align*}
\cos(2x) &= 1 - 2\sin^2 x.
\end{align*}
Rewriting our equation in powers of $\sin^2\theta$ gives:
\begin{align*}
3\sin\theta &= \cos^7\theta - 14\cos^5\theta + 56\cos^3\theta - 70\cos\theta = \\
&= (\underbrace{1 - 2\sin^2\theta}_{\cos^2\theta})^3 - 14(\underbrace{1 - 2\sin^2\theta}_{\cos^2\theta})^2 + 56(\underbrace{1 - 2\sin^2\theta}_{\cos^2\theta}) - 70(\underbrace{\sqrt{1 - \sin^2\theta}}_{\cos\theta}) = \\
&= (1 - 2\sin^2\theta)^3 - 14(1 - 2\sin^2\theta)^2 + 56(1 - 2\sin^2\theta) - 70\sqrt{1 - \sin^2\theta}.
\end{align*}
Our equation is a polynomial equation in powers of $\sin^2\theta$. You can use software like WolframAlpha to find the value or approximate the value of $\sin^2\theta.$ (If you don't have access to external software, you can use numerical methods like the Babylonian method or bisection method to find an approximate solution).

