To solve for the side length of the new cube, we know that the volume of a cube is given by the formula $ V = s^3 $, where $ s $ is the length of one side of the cube. We are given that the volume of the new cube is 19 cubic centimeters and the original cube has a volume of 8 cubic centimeters.

First, let's find the volume of the original cube:
\begin{align}
V_{original} &= 8 \text{ cubic cm} \\
&= (\text{side length})^3 \quad \text{(since it's a cube)} \\
8 &= s_{original}^3
\end{align}

We want to find the side length of the new cube, which has a volume of 19 cubic centimeters, so:

\begin{align}
V_{new} &= 19 \text{ cubic cm} \\
&= (\text{new side length})^3 \\
19 &= s_{new}^3
\end{align}

Now, we have two isolated cubic equations, and we want to find the absolute difference between the side lengths of the new and original cubes:

\begin{align}
8 &= s_{original}^3 \\
19 &= s_{new}^3
\end{align}

We find the difference as the difference of the two equations:

\begin{align}
11 &= s_{new}^3 - s_{original}^3 \\
s_{new} - s_{original} &= (s_{new}^3)^{1/3} - (s_{original}^3)^{1/3}
\end{align}

Since we're looking for the absolute difference, we take the absolute value:

\begin{align}
|s_{new} - s_{original}| &= | (s_{new}^3)^{1/3} - (s_{original}^3)^{1/3} | \\
&= \big| (s_{new}/2)^{3/3} * (s_{new}/2)^{1/3} \cdot 2^{1/3} \\
&-\ (s_{original}/2)^{3/3} \cdot (s_{original}/2)^{1/3} \cdot 2^{1/3} \big|
\end{align}

The expression inside the absolute value sign can be factorized from the distances between the two side lengths:

\begin{align}
|s_{new} - s_{original}| &= \big| (s_{new}/s_{original})^2 \cdot s_{original}/s_{original} - 1 \big| \\
&= \big| (s_{new}/s_{original})^2 - 1 \big| \cdot s_{original}/s_{original}
\end{align}

Note that $(s_{new}/s_{original})^2$ is undefined due to our approximation of taking the cube root earlier in our calculations. To avoid this, we'll consider the relative error from the difference of the cubes' volumes:

\begin{align}
\text{relative error} &= \frac{V_{new} - V_{original}}{V_{original}} \\
&= \frac{19 - 8}{8} \\
&= 11/8
\end{align}

Now, let's assume $\text{relative error} = \delta$. Then we have:

\begin{align}
(s_{new}/s_{original})^3 &= (V_{new} / V_{original}) \\
(s_{new}/s_{original}) ^ 3 - 1 &= \delta
\end{align}

Take the cube root of both sides and apply the approximation:

\begin{align}
s_{new}/s_{original} &= 1 + \delta^{1/3} \\
s_{new}/s_{original} &\approx 1 + (11/8)^{1/3} \\
\frac{s_{new}}{s_{original}} - 1 &\approx (1 + (11/8)^{1/3}) - 1 \\
&\approx (1 +1.1884484)\cdot 0.354 \\
&\approx 0.725
\end{align}

Then, we take the reciprocal to get the ratio of the new and original side lengths:

\begin{align}
\frac{s_{new}}{s_{original}} &\approx \frac{1}{0.725} \\
&\approx 1.374
\end{align
Now, multiply by the original side length to find the new side length:
$s_{new} \approx 1.374 \times 2 \approx 2.748 \text{ cm}$

The absolute difference between the side lengths is $s_{new} - s_{original} \approx 2.748 - 2 = \boxed{0.748 \text{ cm}}$.

